TP.register({id:"lp2-g5",lp:2,grade:"5",mode:"curriculum",checks:1,nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const STORY_TITLE="The Stuck Press";const STORY=["On harvest Saturday, the line at Hollow Creek Orchard stretched past the barn. Everyone wanted fresh cider, and Priya was in charge of the old wooden press. Mrs. Okafor, who owned the orchard, had shown her how to work it only once.","For the first hour, everything went well. Priya dumped chopped apples into the tub, turned the iron handle, and watched golden juice run into the jug. Then, with twelve people still waiting, the handle stopped. Priya pushed harder. It would not move.","Her face grew hot. A man near the front checked his watch. Priya wanted to run and find Mrs. Okafor, but the farm stand was all the way across the field. Instead, she knelt down and looked under the tub. A thick plug of apple skins was jammed in the spout.","Priya grabbed a wooden spoon and scraped the skins loose. Juice gushed out so fast that it splashed her shoes. The people in line clapped.","Later, Mrs. Okafor walked over with a clipboard. “I heard the press quit on you,” she said.","“It did,” said Priya. “So I fixed it.”","Mrs. Okafor looked at Priya’s sticky shoes and laughed. Then she wrote Priya’s name at the top of next Saturday’s list."].join("\n\n");const MORNING_TITLE="Orchard Morning";const MORNING="Before the sun was all the way up, Theo carried his basket into the orchard. Fog lay over the grass like a gray wool blanket. Every branch was heavy with fruit, and each apple was a small red lantern in the dim light. Theo climbed the ladder as carefully as a cat on a fence. He picked for an hour without stopping. By then his basket was a boulder on his arm, and his fingers were as stiff as twigs. At last the sun broke through. Bees hummed, a truck rumbled down the lane, and pickers called to each other across the rows. Theo set down his basket and bit into the last apple he had picked. It was as cold as creek water.";const GRAFT_TITLE="Two Trees in One";const GRAFT=["If you plant a seed from a Gala apple, you will not get a Gala tree. An apple seed is a mix of two parent trees, so the new tree grows fruit that is different from both. Most of the time, that fruit is small and sour.","So how do growers get thousands of trees with the same sweet apples? They use a method called grafting. First, a grower cuts a young twig from a tree with good fruit. This twig is called a scion. Next, the grower cuts off the top of a young tree that has strong roots. The part that is left is called the rootstock. The scion is fitted onto the cut stem, and the two pieces are wrapped tightly with tape. In a few weeks, they grow together into one tree.","The top of the new tree is a copy of the tree the twig came from, so it grows the same kind of apples. The rootstock decides other things, such as how tall the tree will get. Many growers choose rootstock that keeps trees short. Short trees are easier to pick, and more of them fit in a row.","Grafting is not new. Farmers have joined trees this way for more than two thousand years."].join("\n\n");const FROST=[["Text A: A Cold Snap in Spring","Apple trees bloom in spring. If the air drops below about 28 degrees Fahrenheit, open blossoms can freeze and die. A blossom that dies cannot become an apple. One cold night can wipe out most of a year’s crop, so growers watch the weather closely while the trees are in bloom."],["Text B: Giant Fans","On a still, clear night, the coldest air sinks to the ground. Warmer air floats above the treetops. Some growers use wind machines, which are tall fans on towers. The fans pull the warmer air down and mix it with the cold air around the trees. This can warm the orchard by a few degrees."],["Text C: A Coat of Ice","It sounds strange, but some growers spray water on their trees on freezing nights. When water turns to ice, it gives off a little heat. As long as the sprinklers keep running and new ice keeps forming, the blossoms inside the ice stay at about 32 degrees. That is cold, but not cold enough to kill them."]];const SOURCES=[["Source 1","<i>The Orchard Year</i>, a book by Dana Whitfield","Apples keep breathing after they are picked. They take in oxygen and slowly get softer. To slow this down, growers store apples in huge cold rooms that are kept just above freezing."],["Source 2","“Why Apples Last,” a web article by Luis Ortega","In some storage rooms, workers also pump out most of the oxygen. With little oxygen and cold air, the apples almost go to sleep. An apple picked in October can still be crisp the next summer."]];function group(inner,label){return`<div style="text-align:center"><div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;align-items:center">${inner}</div><p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`}function pvFig(heads,digits){const th=h=>`<th style="padding:6px 10px;font-size:1rem">${h}</th>`;const td=d=>`<td style="padding:4px 10px;font-size:2.4rem;font-weight:700;text-align:center;font-family:var(--font-display)">${d}</td>`;return`<table class="chart" style="margin:0 auto"><tr>${heads.map(th).join("")}</tr><tr>${digits.map(td).join("")}</tr></table>`}function tag(name,val,icon){return`<div style="border:3px solid var(--gold-ink);border-radius:16px;padding:8px 22px;background:var(--surface);text-align:center;min-width:150px"><div style="line-height:1.2" aria-hidden="true">${F.icon(icon,40)}</div><div class="fig-label" style="margin:0">${name}</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:700;font-family:var(--font-display);color:var(--ink)">${val}</div></div>`}function pie(){let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 120 120" width="120" role="img" aria-label="One whole apple pie"><circle cx="60" cy="60" r="54" fill="var(--gold)" stroke="var(--gold-ink)" stroke-width="5"/><circle cx="60" cy="60" r="42" fill="none" stroke="var(--gold-ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>`;[[60,44],[46,66],[74,66]].forEach(([x,y])=>{s+=`<path d="M${x-7} ${y+5} q7 -14 14 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--gold-ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>`});return s+"</svg>"}const person=`<span style="display:inline-block;margin-right:8px">${F.icon("face",60)||"👤"}</span>`;const people=n=>`<span style="font-size:3.2rem;letter-spacing:8px" role="img" aria-label="${n} friends">${person.repeat(n)}</span>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=items=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${items.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.25rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:8px 18px;margin:14px auto 0;max-width:820px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const frostHtml=()=>`<div class="texts">${FROST.map(t=>`<article><h3>${t[0]}</h3><p>${t[1]}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const sourcesHtml=()=>`<div class="texts">${SOURCES.map(t=>`<article><h3>${t[0]}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t[1]}</p><p>${t[2]}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const STORAGE="Here is how an apple can still taste fresh in April. After harvest, apples go into a sealed room called controlled atmosphere storage. Machines pull most of the oxygen out of the room and replace it with nitrogen, and the air is held just above freezing. With so little oxygen, the apple ripens very slowly, almost as if it were asleep. A room is filled, sealed, and left shut for months. When a grower is ready to sell that fruit, the room is opened and the apples wake up and ripen in a few days. Not every kind of apple keeps well this way, so growers choose firm varieties for the longest storage.";const BIGWORDS=["horticulture","pollination","irreplaceable","refrigeration","commercially","unmistakably","grantification","prelunctive"];const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Cider Press Read",short:"Press Read",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:110,dir:"Read the story out loud at your normal speed. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute, mark the last word read, and count errors on your own copy. Let the student finish the story.","Fall grade 5 benchmark: about 121 words correct per minute (50th percentile).","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for a change in voice when Mrs. Okafor and Priya talk, and for self-corrections that show the student is reading for meaning."]}]},{id:2,title:"Count the Crop",short:"Crop Count",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],dur:100,dir:"Look at the place value charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing the value of the same digit in two places (10 times as much and 1/10)",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:40px;align-items:flex-start">${group(pvFig(["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones"],["8","8","8","0"]),"Chart A: apples picked this week")}${group(pvFig(["Ones",".","Tenths","Hundredths"],["0",".","7","7"]),"Chart B")}</div>`,qs:["Chart A: What is the value of the 8 in the hundreds place? What is the value of the 8 in the tens place?","The 8 in the hundreds place is worth how many times as much as the 8 in the tens place?","Finish the sentence with a fraction: The 8 in the tens place is worth ____ of the 8 in the hundreds place.","Chart B: True or false? The 7 in the tenths place is worth 10 times as much as the 7 in the hundredths place. Tell how you know."],key:["800 and 80. Both values are needed. Partial: one of the two values given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","10 times as much. 800 is 10 × 80. Partial: naming 10 with no equation and no reason is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","1/10, or one tenth. 80 is 1/10 of 800. Partial: 10 times as much reverses the comparison and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","True. 0.7 is 10 times 0.07, because 7 tenths is the same as 70 hundredths. Each place is worth 10 times the place to its right. Partial: true with no reason from the places is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"The Stuck Press",short:"Stuck Press",parts:[{std:["RL.5.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the story that prove each answer.",desc:"quoting the story accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true),qs:["What made the handle stop? Read me the sentence that tells you.","Why did Priya not go to get Mrs. Okafor? Read me the exact words.","How did Priya feel when the handle stopped? The story does not say it in one word. Read me the words that prove your answer.","What does Mrs. Okafor think of the way Priya handled the problem? Read me the sentence that proves it."],key:["Apple skins were stuck in the spout. Quotes “A thick plug of apple skins was jammed in the spout.” For every answer, the student should read the words as written, not retell them loosely. (RL.5.1, explicit)","Mrs. Okafor was too far away. Quotes “the farm stand was all the way across the field.” (RL.5.1, explicit)","Embarrassed, nervous, or worried. Quotes “Her face grew hot,” “A man near the front checked his watch,” or “Priya wanted to run and find Mrs. Okafor.” (RL.5.1, inference)","She is pleased, or she trusts Priya to run the press again. Quotes “Then she wrote Priya’s name at the top of next Saturday’s list.” Also accept the sentence where she laughs. (RL.5.1, inference)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Crate Stacker",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.3"],dur:110,dir:"Look at the stacks of cubes. Answer out loud.",desc:"counting unit cubes to measure volume and telling what volume means",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:50px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.prism(3,2,3,{unit:40}),"Stack A")}${group(F.prism(5,2,2,{unit:40}),"Stack B")}</div>`,qs:["Each small cube is 1 cubic unit. The stacks are solid, with no gaps inside. What is the volume of Stack A? Say the units.","Which stack has the greater volume, A or B? Tell how you know.","What does volume measure?","Which one would you measure in cubic units? The length of a ladder. The top of a table. Or the space inside an apple crate.","Spot the mistake. Nora drops 20 cubes into a box and leaves gaps between them. She says, “The volume of the box is 20 cubic units.” Is she right? Tell why."],key:["18 cubic units. Stack A is 3 long, 2 wide, and 3 tall. Or: count the cubes one layer at a time, 6 and 6 and 6. Partial: 18 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","Stack B. It has 20 cubes, so its volume is 20 cubic units, and 20 is more than 18. Partial: naming Stack B with no counts given is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","The amount of space a solid figure takes up, or how many unit cubes it takes to fill it. Partial: naming the space with nothing said about unit cubes is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","The space inside an apple crate. A ladder’s length is measured in units of length, and a table top is measured in square units. Partial: the right choice with nothing said about the other two is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","No. To measure volume, the unit cubes must be packed with no gaps and no overlaps. The box holds more than 20 cubes, so its volume is more than 20 cubic units. Partial: saying no with nothing said about gaps and overlaps is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Farm Stand Signs",short:"Sign Fixer",parts:[{std:["L.5.1"],dur:75,dir:"Each sign has one mistake. Say the whole sentence the right way.",desc:"fixing spoken grammar mistakes (verb tense shifts, perfect tenses, either/or)",qs:["Yesterday we pick apples and carried them to the barn.","The bees have flew back to the hive.","By the time the truck arrived, we have filled forty crates.","You can buy either a jug of cider nor a bag of apples.","She climbs the ladder, fills her basket, and walked back to the cart."],key:["Yesterday we picked apples and carried them to the barn. (L.5.1, fixing a shift in verb tense)","The bees have flown back to the hive. (L.5.1, present perfect tense)","By the time the truck arrived, we had filled forty crates. (L.5.1, past perfect tense)","You can buy either a jug of cider or a bag of apples. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions either/or)","She climbs the ladder, fills her basket, and walks back to the cart. Also correct: climbed, filled, and walked. (L.5.1, fixing a shift in verb tense)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Weigh Station",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],dur:105,dir:"Say number 1 out loud. Write the rest on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading, writing, and comparing decimals to thousandths",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:24px">${tag("Apple A","0.25 kg","apple")}${tag("Apple B","0.205 kg","apple")}${tag("Apple C","0.3 kg","apple")}</div>`,qs:["Read the weight of Apple B out loud.","Write this number: three and sixteen thousandths.","Write 5.408 in expanded form.","Which is heavier, Apple A or Apple B? Write it with &gt; or &lt;. Tell how you know."],key:["Two hundred five thousandths of a kilogram. If the student says “zero point two zero five,” ask, “Can you say it with its place value name?” Partial: zero point two zero five is approaching, since the place value name is what is asked. (5.NBT.A.3, read)","3.016. Partial: 3.16 leaves the hundredths place out and does not count. (5.NBT.A.3, write)","5 + 0.4 + 0.008, or 5 × 1 + 4 × (1/10) + 8 × (1/1000). There are no hundredths. Partial: writing a hundredths term is approaching, since there are none. (5.NBT.A.3, expanded form)","Apple A. 0.25 &gt; 0.205, or 0.205 &lt; 0.25. Both have 2 tenths, but A has 5 hundredths and B has 0 hundredths. Also correct: 250 thousandths is more than 205 thousandths. Partial: the right apple with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3, compare)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Orchard Morning",short:"Word Pictures",parts:[{std:["RL.5.4"],dur:100,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud.",desc:"explaining similes, metaphors, and words as they are used in a story",html:()=>F.passage(MORNING,MORNING_TITLE,true),qs:["“Fog lay over the grass like a gray wool blanket.” What does this tell you about the fog? Is it a simile or a metaphor? How do you know?","“Each apple was a small red lantern in the dim light.” Are the apples really lanterns? What does the author mean?","“His basket was a boulder on his arm.” What does this tell you about the basket?","What does the word dim mean in this story? Which words in the story help you know?"],key:["The fog was thick and low and covered the grass. It is a simile because it compares two things with the word like. (RL.5.4, simile)","No. It is a metaphor. The apples were bright red and stood out, almost glowing, in the low light. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","The basket was very heavy because it was full of apples. He had picked for an hour. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","Not bright, or a little dark. Clues: “Before the sun was all the way up” and the fog. Later “the sun broke through.” (RL.5.4, meaning of a word as used in the text)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Pie Shares",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.3"],dur:140,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"fractions as division, with sharing problems that have fraction and mixed number answers",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:50px">${group(pie()+pie(),"2 apple pies")}${group(people(3),"3 friends")}</div>`,qs:["Look at the picture. 2 apple pies are shared equally by 3 friends. How much pie does each friend get?","Write a division equation for number 1.","The fraction 5/8 is the answer to what division problem?","7 liters of cider are poured equally into 4 jugs. How many liters are in each jug? Between what two whole numbers is your answer?","True or false? 4 ÷ 5 is the same as 5/4. Tell how you know."],key:["2/3 of a pie. Partial: 3/2 reverses the share and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","2 ÷ 3 = 2/3. Partial: 3 ÷ 2 reverses the division and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","5 ÷ 8, or 5 things shared equally among 8. Partial: 8 ÷ 5 reverses the division and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","7/4 liters, which is 1 3/4 liters. It is between 1 and 2. Partial: 7/4 right with no pair of whole numbers named is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","False. 4 ÷ 5 is 4/5, which is less than 1 whole. 5/4 is 5 ÷ 4, which is more than 1 whole. Partial: false with no comparison to 1 whole is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Two Trees in One",short:"Two in One",parts:[{std:["RI.5.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the text. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the text that prove each answer.",desc:"quoting an informational text accurately to tell what it says and to back up inferences",html:()=>F.passage(GRAFT,GRAFT_TITLE,true),qs:["What is a scion? Read me the words that tell you.","Why do many growers choose rootstock that keeps trees short? Read me the sentence.","A grower wants 500 new Gala trees. Should the grower plant Gala seeds? Read me the sentence that proves your answer.","A grafted tree is made from two trees. Which part decides what kind of apples it grows? Read me the sentence that proves it."],key:["A young twig cut from a tree with good fruit. Quotes “First, a grower cuts a young twig from a tree with good fruit. This twig is called a scion.” For every answer, the student should read the words as written, not retell them loosely. (RI.5.1, explicit)","Short trees are easier to work with. Quotes “Short trees are easier to pick, and more of them fit in a row.” (RI.5.1, explicit)","No. Quotes “If you plant a seed from a Gala apple, you will not get a Gala tree,” or “Most of the time, that fruit is small and sour.” (RI.5.1, inference)","The top part, which comes from the scion. Quotes “The top of the new tree is a copy of the tree the twig came from, so it grows the same kind of apples.” (RI.5.1, inference)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Bushels and Bins",short:"Bins",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.1"],dur:110,dir:"Answer out loud. For number 5, write your number on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"using the 10 times and 1/10 pattern between places in whole numbers and decimals",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${group(pvFig(["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones"],["4","6","6","4"]),"Apples in the big bin")}</div>`,qs:["A small crate holds 40 apples. A truck bin holds 400 apples. 400 is how many times as much as 40?","0.9 is 10 times as much as what number?","0.05 is 1/10 of what number?","Spot the mistake. Dev looks at the chart and says, “The two 6s are worth the same because they are both 6.” Is Dev right? Tell why.","Write a number that has a 4 worth 1/10 as much as the 4 in 3,410."],key:["10 times as much. Partial: 360 more is a difference and not a comparison of times as much, so it does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","0.09. Partial: 9 goes the wrong way along the places and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","0.5. Partial: 0.005 goes the wrong way along the places and does not count. (5.NBT.A.1)","No. The 6 in the hundreds place is worth 600 and the 6 in the tens place is worth 60. The one on the left is worth 10 times as much, and the one on the right is worth 1/10 as much. Partial: saying no with only one of the two values given is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)","The 4 in 3,410 is worth 400, and 1/10 of 400 is 40. Any number with a 4 in the tens place is correct, such as 40, 147, or 2,845. Partial: a number with the 4 in the hundreds place is approaching. (5.NBT.A.1)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Farm Stand Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"Teach a reader about apples"},std:["W.5.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to teach a reader about apples. Use at least three facts from the fact box. Put them in an order that makes sense. Write 5 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: apples",["There are more than 7,000 kinds of apples in the world.","A young apple tree needs several years, often 4 or 5, before it grows fruit.","About one fourth of an apple is air. That is why apples float.","Most apples are still picked by hand so they do not bruise."])}${checklist(["Start by naming your topic","Put facts that go together next to each other","Use linking words: also, another, for example, because","End with a closing sentence"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly (W.5.2)","Groups related information in an order that makes sense (W.5.2)","Develops the topic with at least three facts or details from the fact box, written in the student’s own sentences (W.5.2)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as also, another, for example, because, in contrast (W.5.2)","Ends with a concluding sentence (W.5.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"A surprise at the top of the ladder"},std:["W.5.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:300,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You are at the top of the ladder, reaching for an apple, when you find something in the tree that should not be there. Write the story of what happens. It can be real or made up. Write 5 to 8 sentences.</p>${pills(["First","Then","After that","Finally"])}${checklist(["Tell who is in the story and where it happens","Tell the events in order","Show what someone says, thinks, or feels","Use details a reader can see, hear, or feel","Give your story an ending"])}`,key:["Orients the reader: sets up the situation and introduces the narrator or characters (W.5.3)","Events unfold in a clear order, with transition words such as first, later, after that, finally (W.5.3)","Uses dialogue or description to show what happens and how the characters respond (W.5.3)","Uses concrete words and sensory details (W.5.3)","Has an ending that follows from the events (W.5.3)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Pack the Shed",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.5"],dur:150,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",desc:"finding volume by multiplying edge lengths, by base times height, and by adding two volumes",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${group(F.prism(4,3,2,{unit:40}),"A box of cider jugs, shown with unit cubes")}</div>`,qs:["Look at the box. How many cubes are in the bottom layer? How many layers are there? What is the volume?","Write a multiplication equation with three numbers for the volume of the box in the picture.","A crate is 5 feet long, 2 feet wide, and 3 feet tall. What is its volume?","The floor of a storage bin has an area of 20 square feet. The bin is 4 feet tall. What is its volume?","A farm stand counter is made of two boxes pushed together. One box is 3 by 2 by 4. The other box is 2 by 2 by 5. What is the total volume? Tell how you found it."],key:["12 cubes in the bottom layer and 2 layers. 12 × 2 = 24 cubic units (or 12 + 12). Partial: two of the three parts answered is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","4 × 3 × 2 = 24, in any order. Partial: 24 given with no three-number equation is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","30 cubic feet, from 5 × 2 × 3. Or: 5 × 2 = 10 for the base, then 10 × 3. Partial: 30 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","80 cubic feet, from 20 × 4. Volume is the area of the base times the height. Partial: 80 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5)","44 cubic units. The first box is 24 and the second is 20. Find each volume, then add: 24 + 20 = 44. Partial: one of the two boxes worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5, volume is additive)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Frost Fighters",parts:[{std:["RI.5.9"],dur:150,dir:"Read all three texts. Then answer out loud. Use facts from more than one text.",desc:"putting together information from three short texts on the same topic",html:()=>frostHtml(),qs:["Text B and Text C each tell one way growers protect apple blossoms. Name both ways.","Use Text A and Text C together. Why does a coat of ice keep the blossoms alive?","Be the expert. In 3 or 4 sentences, tell why a freezing spring night is a problem and what growers can do about it. Use facts from at least two texts."],key:["Wind machines (tall fans that pull warmer air down) from Text B, and spraying water so that ice forms on the trees from Text C. Both ways are needed. (RI.5.9)","Text A says open blossoms can freeze and die below about 28 degrees. Text C says blossoms inside the ice stay at about 32 degrees. 32 is warmer than 28, so the blossoms live. The answer needs a fact from each text. (RI.5.9)","The talk gives the problem from Text A (blossoms can freeze below about 28 degrees, a dead blossom cannot become an apple, one night can wipe out most of a crop) and at least one fix from Text B or Text C with how it works. Facts are correct and come from two or more texts. (RI.5.9)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Cider Jug Lineup",short:"Jug Lineup",parts:[{std:["5.NBT.A.3"],dur:125,dir:"Say number 1 out loud. Write the rest on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading, writing, comparing, and ordering decimals to thousandths",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:24px">${tag("Jug 1","0.52 L","milk")}${tag("Jug 2","0.508 L","milk")}${tag("Jug 3","0.6 L","milk")}</div>`,qs:["Read this number out loud: 12.06","Write this number: nine hundred four thousandths.","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = for each pair. First pair: 0.7 and 0.70. Second pair: 1.09 and 1.1.","Put the three jugs in order from the least cider to the most cider.","Spot the mistake. Max says, “0.125 is greater than 0.5 because 125 is greater than 5.” Is Max right? Tell how you know."],key:["Twelve and six hundredths. Partial: twelve point zero six is approaching, since the place value name is what is asked. (5.NBT.A.3, read)","0.904. Partial: 900.004 reads the words as two separate numbers and does not count. (5.NBT.A.3, write)","0.7 = 0.70 and 1.09 &lt; 1.1. Both are needed. Partial: one of the two pairs right is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3, compare)","Jug 2 (0.508 L), then Jug 1 (0.52 L), then Jug 3 (0.6 L). Or: line the three up as thousandths, 508, 520, 600. Partial: two of the three in order is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3, compare)","No. 0.5 is 5 tenths, and 0.125 has only 1 tenth, so 0.5 is greater. Also correct: 0.5 is 500 thousandths and 0.125 is 125 thousandths. Partial: saying no with no reason from the places is approaching. (5.NBT.A.3, compare)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Busy Bee Words",parts:[{std:["L.5.1"],dur:75,dir:"Every word has a job. Answer out loud.",desc:"spoken grammar items (interjections, prepositions, conjunctions, future perfect, neither/nor)",qs:["“Wow, that basket is heavy!” Which word is the interjection? What does it show?","“The ladder leans against the tree.” Which word is the preposition? What does it tell you?","“We wanted to pick more apples, but it started to rain.” Which word is the conjunction? What does it join?","Say the sentence with the right form of the verb pick: “By next Friday, the workers ____ every tree in this row.”","Make one sentence with neither and nor: “The cider is not too sweet. The cider is not too sour.”"],key:["Wow. It shows a strong feeling, such as surprise. (L.5.1, interjection)","against. It tells where the ladder is: how the ladder and the tree go together. (L.5.1, preposition)","but. It joins two complete thoughts and shows that the second one goes against the first. (L.5.1, conjunction)","By next Friday, the workers will have picked every tree in this row. (L.5.1, future perfect tense)","The cider is neither too sweet nor too sour. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions neither/nor)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Apple Notes",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:150,dir:"Read the two sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"notes in the student’s own words from two sources, with a list of sources",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Question: How do apples stay fresh for months after they are picked?</p>${sourcesHtml()}<ol class="qlist"><li>Write 3 short notes that answer the question. Use your own words. A note is a few words, not a whole sentence.</li><li>Put a 1 or a 2 next to each note to show which source it came from.</li><li>Under your notes, list your sources. Write the title and the author of each one.</li></ol>`,key:["Writes 3 notes that answer the question, such as: apples keep taking in oxygen and get softer, cold rooms just above freezing slow this down, most of the oxygen is pumped out, October apples stay crisp until the next summer. (W.5.8, gathers relevant information)","Notes are short and in the student’s own words, not sentences copied from the sources. (W.5.8, paraphrases in notes)","Each note is marked 1 or 2, and the notes use both sources. (W.5.8)","Lists both sources by title and author: The Orchard Year by Dana Whitfield and “Why Apples Last” by Luis Ortega. (W.5.8, list of sources)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Farm Stand Speech",short:"Speech",parts:[{std:["SL.5.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:115,dir:"Pick one question. Plan for 1 minute. Then give your talk out loud.",desc:"presenting an opinion out loud with reasons, details, and clear speech",html:()=>`<p class="big">Pick one. Tell your opinion in a short talk, about 1 minute long.</p><ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px"><li>Would you rather spend a day picking apples from the top of a ladder or running the cider press?</li><li>What is the best way to eat an apple: fresh, baked in a pie, or cooked into applesauce?</li></ul>${pills(["My opinion","Reason 1 and a detail","Reason 2 and a detail","Closing"])}`,key:["States a clear opinion at the start (SL.5.4)","Gives at least two reasons in an order that makes sense (SL.5.4, sequences ideas logically)","Backs up the reasons with facts, examples, or descriptive details (SL.5.4)","Ends with a closing that goes back to the opinion (SL.5.4)","Speaks clearly at a pace that is easy to follow (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Orchard Word Pick",short:"Big Words",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:BIGWORDS,n:8,dur:45,dir:"Read each word out loud. The last two are made-up words. Break them into parts and sound them out.",desc:"reading unfamiliar multisyllabic words using syllable patterns and word parts",key:["Count a word correct when the student reads it right the first time or fixes it without help (RF.5.3)","Watch the parts: horti-culture, pollin-ation, ir-replace-able, re-friger-ation, commerci-ally, un-mistak-ably","The last two are made up: gran-ti-fi-ca-tion and pre-lunc-tive. They show whether the student uses syllable patterns and endings or guesses from the first letters (RF.5.3)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Sleeping Apples",short:"Storage",parts:[{std:["SL.5.2"],n:3,dur:45,read:STORAGE,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads about apple storage. It is not on your screen. Then summarize it out loud.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center">Listen only. Your teacher will read this once.</p>`,qs:["Summarize what you just heard in two or three sentences.","What is the one idea the whole thing is about?","Name one detail you left out of your summary on purpose. Then say why it did not belong in it."],key:["A good summary says that apples are kept in a sealed room with most of the oxygen replaced by nitrogen and the air just above freezing, which makes them ripen very slowly, so they stay fresh for months until the room is opened (SL.5.2)","How growers keep apples fresh for months after harvest (SL.5.2)","Any real detail dropped for a real reason, such as the name controlled atmosphere storage, or that firm varieties keep longest, because a summary keeps the main idea and leaves out the small details (SL.5.2)","Retelling every sentence in order is not a summary. Note it if the student does that."]}]},{id:20,title:"Weigh In With an Opinion",short:"Opinion",parts:[{std:["W.5.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion piece with a clear position, ordered reasons supported by facts, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Hollow Creek is planting a new row. Should Mrs. Okafor choose rootstock that keeps the trees short, or let them grow tall? Write your opinion with two reasons. Write 4 to 6 sentences.</p>${factBox("Facts you may use",["Short trees are easier to pick, and more of them fit in a row.","A tall tree can grow more fruit on one trunk.","Picking from the top of a tall ladder is slower and harder.","Rootstock is the part that decides how tall the tree gets."])}${checklist(["Say your opinion in the first sentence","Give two reasons, one at a time","Back each reason with a fact","End with a conclusion that goes back to your opinion"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states an opinion clearly (W.5.1)","Gives reasons in a logical order, each one grouped with what supports it (W.5.1)","Supports the reasons with facts from the box or from what the student knows (W.5.1)","Ends with a concluding statement that ties back to the opinion (W.5.1)","Either side is correct. Rate the structure and the support, not which one the student picks."]}]},{id:21,title:"Order of Work",short:"Expressions",parts:[{std:["5.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Show your work on your paper.",desc:"writing and evaluating numerical expressions with parentheses",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center">3 × (14 + 6)</p>`,qs:["What does this expression equal?","Where do the parentheses change the answer? What would 3 × 14 + 6 equal instead?","Write an expression for this, but do not work it out: add 8 and 7, then multiply the result by 4."],key:["60. Inside the parentheses first: 14 + 6 = 20, then 3 × 20 = 60. Partial: 48 comes from multiplying before adding and does not count. (5.OA.A.1)","3 × 14 + 6 = 48. Without the parentheses the multiplying happens first, so the answer drops from 60 to 48. Partial: 48 found with nothing said about why the answer changes is approaching. (5.OA.A.1)","4 × (8 + 7), or (8 + 7) × 4. The parentheses are the point here. A student who also writes 60 has answered a question that was not asked. Partial: 4 × 8 + 7 leaves the parentheses out and does not count. (5.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Cider by the Fraction",short:"Fractions",parts:[{title:"Add and Take Away",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Solve on your paper. Say your answers out loud.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions with unlike denominators in a word problem, and estimating with benchmarks",fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:10px">${F.fracBar(2,1,{width:340})}${F.fracBar(3,1,{width:340})}</div>`,qs:["Priya poured 1/2 of a gallon of cider into one jug and 1/3 of a gallon into another. How much cider is that in all?","A third jug held 3/4 of a gallon. She sold 2/3 of a gallon from it. How much is left?","Before you worked the second one out, was the answer going to be closer to 0, to 1/2, or to 1? How could you tell?"],key:["5/6 of a gallon. 1/2 = 3/6 and 1/3 = 2/6, so 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. Or: twelfths work too, 6/12 + 4/12 = 10/12 = 5/6. Partial: 2/5 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1, 5.NF.A.2)","1/12 of a gallon. 3/4 = 9/12 and 2/3 = 8/12, so 9/12 - 8/12 = 1/12. Partial: 1/1 comes from subtracting across and does not count. (5.NF.A.1, 5.NF.A.2)","Closer to 0. 3/4 and 2/3 are both a little more than 1/2, so taking one from the other leaves only a sliver. An answer like 1/12 fits; an answer like 1/2 would not. Partial: naming closer to 0 with no benchmark reason is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, benchmark estimation)","The two bars on the screen are halves and thirds, drawn the same length, so they can be used to show why sixths work for the first one."]},{title:"A Fraction of a Fraction",std:["5.NF.B.4"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the rectangle. Answer out loud.",desc:"multiplying a fraction by a whole number and by another fraction, with an area model",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.gridRect(4,5,0,{cell:46})}</div>`,qs:["What is 3/4 of 20?","The rectangle has 4 rows and 5 columns. Shade 1/2 of 3/5 of it in your head. What fraction of the whole rectangle is that?","What is 2/3 × 4/5?"],key:["15. 3/4 × 20 = 60/4 = 15. Or: 20 ÷ 4 = 5, then 5 × 3 = 15. (5.NF.B.4)","3/10. Three of the five columns is 3/5, and half of those columns is 3/10 of the whole. On this 4 by 5 grid that is 6 of the 20 squares. Partial: 3/5 given with no half taken is approaching. (5.NF.B.4)","8/15. Or: a 3 by 5 area model with 8 of the 15 squares shaded. (5.NF.B.4)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Map the Rows",short:"Grid",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the orchard map. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a coordinate grid: the origin, the two axes, and ordered pairs",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.coordGrid({max:8,size:330,xLabel:"east",yLabel:"north",points:[[3,5,"Barn"],[6,2,"Press"]]})}</div>`,qs:["What is the ordered pair for the barn?","What is the ordered pair for the press?","Start at the origin. Say in words how to travel to the press. Then say which number in the pair tells you each move."],key:["(3, 5). Partial: (5, 3) reverses the pair and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","(6, 2). Partial: (2, 6) reverses the pair and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","From (0, 0) go 6 to the right along the x axis, then 2 up along the y axis. The first number is the move along the x axis and the second is the move along the y axis. Partial: the route right with nothing said about which number is which move is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Reversing the pair is the usual slip. If the student says (5, 3) for the barn, note that the order of the two numbers is the thing to work on."]}]},{id:24,title:"Too Small to See",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the picture. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A drop of red food colouring goes into a jug of clear apple juice. Nobody stirs it. By lunchtime the whole jug is pale pink.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("drop",84)}${F.icon("apple",84)}</div>`,qs:["Nobody stirred the jug. Explain how the colour reached every part of the juice.","A sealed bag of air weighs more than the same bag with the air squeezed out. What does that tell you about air?","Why can you see the colour spread but not see the particles themselves?"],key:["The colouring is made of particles far too small to see. They move on their own and spread out into the spaces between the juice particles until they are mixed all through. (5-PS1-1)","Air is matter. It is made of particles that have weight, even though the air looks like nothing at all. (5-PS1-1)","Each particle is far too small for an eye to pick out. You see the colour only once enormous numbers of them have spread through the juice. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four scraps turned up in the orchard shed. All four are grey. The crew tested each one.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Scrap</th><th>Mass of a 10 cubic cm piece</th><th>Bends without snapping?</th><th>Conducts electricity?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>J</td><td>7 g</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>K</td><td>27 g</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>L</td><td>79 g</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>M</td><td>25 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four materials are grey rubber, aluminium, cast iron, and concrete.</p>`,qs:["Which scrap is the rubber? Name the two test results that told you.","Scraps K and L both conduct electricity. Which is the cast iron, and which measurement separates them?","A worker says the grey colour tells you nothing useful. Use the chart to explain why that is right."],key:["J. It is the only one that both bends and does not conduct electricity, and it is by far the lightest for its size. (5-PS1-3)","L is the cast iron. The mass of the same size piece separates them: 79 g against 27 g for the aluminium. (5-PS1-3)","All four scraps are grey, yet their mass, flexibility, and conductivity are all different. Measured properties tell the materials apart; colour does not. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Weighing the Cider",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three sealed cider jugs were weighed, left overnight in different places, and weighed again. None was opened.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed jug</th><th>Where it sat</th><th>Weight at night</th><th>Weight in the morning</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>in the cold store, where it froze solid</td><td>1,050 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>beside the stove, warm all night</td><td>1,050 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>on the counter with a spoon of sugar stirred in</td><td>1,062 g</td><td>1,062 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Jug 1 froze solid. What does it weigh in the morning? Why?","Jug 2 is warm and the inside of the lid is wet. What does it weigh in the morning?","Someone says jug 3 should weigh 1,050 g because the sugar vanished. What is wrong with that?"],key:["1,050 g. Freezing rearranges the particles into a solid but does not add or remove any, and the jug is sealed, so the weight does not change. (5-PS1-2)","1,050 g. Some cider became vapour and condensed on the lid, but all of it is still inside the sealed jug. (5-PS1-2)","The sugar did not vanish. It dissolved, which means it spread out among the liquid particles and is still in the jug, so the weight stays at 1,062 g. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three things were mixed at the cider press and the results written down.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mixing</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>cut apple left in air</td><td>the white flesh turned brown, and no scraping or rinsing brought the white back</td></tr>
     <tr><td>apple juice and clear water</td><td>paler juice, nothing else; boiling the water off left the same juice solids behind</td></tr>
     <tr><td>warm milk and lemon juice</td><td>solid white lumps formed in a thin watery liquid within a minute</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two mixings made a new substance? Give the evidence for each.","Which mixing did not make a new substance, and what is the evidence?","A cut apple brushed with lemon juice stays white much longer. What does that suggest about what turned the apple brown?"],key:["The cut apple in air, because the brown colour is a new substance that cannot be rinsed or scraped away, and the milk with lemon juice, because solid lumps appeared where there had been none. (5-PS1-4)","The juice and water, because boiling the water off gave back the same juice solids. Getting the starting material back shows it was only mixed. (5-PS1-4)","Something in the air, oxygen, reacts with the cut surface to make the brown substance. Lemon juice gets in the way of that reaction, so the change is slowed. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:26,title:"What Builds a Tree",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">An apple tree was planted as a sapling in a measured plot. Twenty years later the tree was weighed, and so was the soil under it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",76)}${F.icon("tree",88)}${F.icon("apple",76)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>At planting</th><th>Twenty years later</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the tree, dried</td><td>3 kg</td><td>410 kg</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the dry soil in the plot</td><td>5,000 kg</td><td>4,985 kg</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How much dry weight did the tree gain? How much did the soil lose?","Where did nearly all of that new wood, bark, leaf, and fruit come from?","A grower adds fertiliser every spring. If the material does not come from the soil, what is the fertiliser for?"],key:["The tree gained 407 kg. The soil lost 15 kg. (5-LS1-1)","From air and water. The tree takes carbon dioxide out of the air and water up through its roots and builds its own material from them, using energy from sunlight. (5-LS1-1)","Fertiliser supplies small amounts of minerals such as nitrogen that a plant needs to work properly. Those are needed in tiny quantities and are not where the bulk of the tree's material comes from. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the chain. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">One path energy takes in the orchard.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("apple",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("mouse",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("owl",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">A mouse eats fallen apples. An owl eats the mouse.</p>`,qs:["The owl flies, hunts, and keeps itself warm all night. Trace that energy back to where it started.","The apple tree is not eating anything. How does it get the energy to make apples?","Explain why a chain like this cannot have more steps than a few."],key:["It came from the mouse, which got it from the apple, which the tree made using energy from sunlight. Every step passes along energy that began as sunlight. (5-PS3-1)","It captures sunlight in its leaves and uses that energy, along with air and water, to make its own food, and some of that food is stored in the fruit. (5-PS3-1)","Each step uses up most of the energy for moving, growing, and staying warm, so only a small part is left to pass on. After a few steps there is not enough left to feed another animal. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"The Orchard Floor",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Windfall apples and old leaves pile up under the trees each autumn. By the next summer the pile is gone. Here is where the matter goes.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>air and soil</td><td>apple tree</td><td>the tree takes in carbon dioxide, water, and minerals</td></tr>
     <tr><td>apple tree</td><td>deer and mice</td><td>they eat fruit and leaves</td></tr>
     <tr><td>windfall apples, leaves, animal waste</td><td>earthworms, fungi, bacteria</td><td>decomposers break the material down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>earthworms, fungi, bacteria</td><td>?</td><td>?</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("leaf",70)}${F.icon("deer",70)}${F.icon("mushroom",70)}${F.icon("snail",70)}</div>`,qs:["Fill in the last row. Where does the matter go after the decomposers have it, and how?","Name the decomposers in this model and say what makes them decomposers.","A grower rakes up every leaf and apple and hauls them off the orchard each autumn. What does the model say will happen to the soil over many years?","Trace one carbon atom from the air into a deer and back out to the air using this model."],key:["Back to the air and the soil. As decomposers break material down they release carbon dioxide into the air and minerals into the soil, where plants can take them up again. (5-LS2-1)","Earthworms, fungi, and bacteria. They feed on dead material and waste and break it into simpler substances rather than eating living plants or animals. (5-LS2-1)","The minerals carried off in the leaves and apples never return, so the soil gets poorer and the trees grow less well unless the grower puts something back. (5-LS2-1)","The tree takes carbon dioxide from the air and builds it into an apple, a deer eats the apple, and the carbon leaves the deer when it breathes out, or later when its waste or body is broken down by decomposers. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Falling Fruit",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">A ripe apple lets go of its branch. A picker's bag on a long strap hangs straight below the shoulder.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("apple",80)}${F.icon("pack",80)}${F.icon("tree",84)}</div>`,qs:["Which way does the apple travel once it lets go? Which way does the hanging bag point?","An apple tree in New Zealand is on the other side of the globe from this one. Which way does an apple fall there, and why is that still called down?","A picker says heavy apples fall and light leaves do not, so gravity only pulls heavy things. What in the orchard shows that is wrong?"],key:["Both go straight down toward the ground. (5-PS2-1)","It falls toward the ground where it is growing, which is toward the centre of Earth. Down always means toward Earth's centre, so down points a different way in space at each spot on the globe. (5-PS2-1)","A leaf does reach the ground, just slowly, because air pushes back on its wide shape. Dropped in a jar with the air pumped out, a leaf and an apple fall together. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">A late frost settles over the orchard. Growers switch on sprinklers, and the thin coat of ice that forms releases a little heat and keeps the buds from freezing. In the lane, cold air pools at the low end and that row of trees loses its blossom.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("snowflake",72)}${F.icon("drop",72)}${F.icon("cherryBlossom",72)}${F.icon("mountain",72)}</div>`,qs:["Name the two spheres interacting when cold air settles onto the blossom.","The growers spray water to protect living buds. Which two spheres are being brought together on purpose?","Cold air pools at the low end of the lane. Which sphere decides where the low spots are, and what is the result for the trees?"],key:["The atmosphere and the biosphere. Cold air from the atmosphere acts on living plants. (5-ESS2-1)","The hydrosphere and the biosphere. Water is used to shield the living buds. The atmosphere is in it too, since the frost came from the air. (5-ESS2-1)","The geosphere, because the shape of the land makes the low spots where heavy cold air collects. The result is that living trees in the low row lose their blossom, which connects geosphere, atmosphere, and biosphere. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Autumn Sky",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the chart and answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two lanterns hang in the orchard lane. Both have the same bulb inside.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Lantern</th><th>Bulb</th><th>Distance from you</th><th>How bright it looks</th></tr>
     <tr><td>near one</td><td>same bulb</td><td>3 m</td><td>bright</td></tr>
     <tr><td>far one</td><td>same bulb</td><td>60 m</td><td>a faint dot</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Now the same idea with stars.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Betelgeuse</td><td>tens of thousands of units</td><td>about 550 light-years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("lantern",76)}${F.icon("sun",76)}${F.icon("star",76)}</div>`,qs:["The two lanterns have identical bulbs. Why does one look so much fainter?","Betelgeuse gives off tens of thousands of times more light than the sun. Why does the sun look so much brighter from the orchard?","Use the lantern lane to explain this in your own words. How bright a star looks does not tell you how much light it really makes. Why not?"],key:["It is much further away. Light spreads out as it travels, so less of it reaches your eye from the far lantern. (5-ESS1-1)","The sun is only 8 light-minutes away and Betelgeuse is about 550 light-years away. That enormous difference in distance more than cancels out the difference in light given off. (5-ESS1-1)","Two identical bulbs looked completely different because of distance alone. So how bright something looks depends on both how much light it makes and how far away it is, and you need the distance before you can judge the light. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">A post at the orchard gate casts a shadow. It was measured at noon on the same date each month, and the hours of daylight were recorded too.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Month</th><th>Noon shadow</th><th>Hours of daylight</th></tr>
     <tr><td>June</td><td>31 cm</td><td>14.6</td></tr>
     <tr><td>August</td><td>58 cm</td><td>13.1</td></tr>
     <tr><td>October</td><td>112 cm</td><td>11.3</td></tr>
     <tr><td>December</td><td>168 cm</td><td>9.7</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern linking the noon shadow and the hours of daylight.","Predict the noon shadow in February compared with December, and say why.","The same stars that stood over the gate in October are not there in June. What causes that?"],key:["As the hours of daylight go down the noon shadow gets longer. The two change together: longest shadow with the shortest day in December, shortest shadow with the longest day in June. (5-ESS1-2)","Shorter than December's 168 cm, because the days are getting longer again after December and the midday sun climbs higher. Between roughly the October and December values is a reasonable estimate. (5-ESS1-2)","Earth travels around the sun over the year, so at night we face a different direction in space in different seasons and different stars are overhead. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Water for the Orchard",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">Imagine all of Earth's water poured into 100 identical buckets.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Reservoir</th><th>Buckets out of 100</th></tr>
     <tr><td>salty ocean</td><td>97</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ice caps and glaciers</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>groundwater</td><td>1</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lakes, rivers, soil, and air</td><td>a splash, far less than one bucket</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Out of the 100 buckets, how many hold water an orchard could irrigate with straight away? Explain.","Turn the bucket model into percentages. What percentage of Earth's water is fresh, and what percentage is fresh and also easy to reach?","If you drew a bar graph of the four reservoirs, describe what a reader would notice first."],key:["About one, the groundwater bucket, plus a splash from lakes and rivers. The 97 ocean buckets are salty and the 2 ice buckets are frozen solid and far away. (5-ESS2-2)","About 3% is fresh. Of that, only about 1% of all water, the groundwater, plus a trace in lakes and rivers is easy to reach, so roughly 1% of all Earth's water. (5-ESS2-2)","One bar almost at the top of the graph for the ocean, and three bars so short they nearly vanish. The surface water bar would be hard to see at all. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two orchard towns were losing soil and water. Each tried a different plan.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Town</th><th>What they did</th><th>Result after two years</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Ridge</td><td>switched from open ditches to drip lines at the base of each tree</td><td>water use down 40%, same size crop</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Hollow</td><td>planted grass and clover between the rows instead of bare earth</td><td>soil washing off cut by two thirds, and more earthworms in the soil</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why does a drip line at the base of a tree use less water than an open ditch?","Planting between the rows cut soil loss. Explain how plants do that.","Combine the two reports into advice for a third town that is short of water and losing soil."],key:["It puts water where the roots are instead of spreading it across open ground, so far less evaporates from the surface or soaks away where no root can reach it. (5-ESS3-1)","Roots hold the soil in place and the leaves and stems slow the rain before it hits the ground, so less soil is loosened and carried away. The cover also lets more water soak in instead of running off. (5-ESS3-1)","Do both: drip lines to cut water use and a planted cover between the rows to hold the soil and let rain soak in. The answer has to use something from each report. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Design an Apple Sorter",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"The orchard sells big apples fresh and sends small ones to the cider press. Right now two workers sort every crate by hand, which takes all afternoon and leaves bruises where apples get dropped. The owner wants something that separates big apples from small ones without bruising them. It has to be built from what is in the barn, and it has to be ready before picking starts in two weeks.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name two things the sorter must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the builders have to work inside."],key:["Sorting apples by size by hand is slow and bruises the fruit, so the orchard needs a way to separate big from small without damage. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two criteria from the story: separates big apples from small ones, does not bruise them, and is faster than two workers sorting by hand. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: it must be made from materials already in the barn, and it must be finished within two weeks. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, tested on the same crate of 200 apples.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Sorted correctly</th><th>Bruised apples</th><th>Crate time</th><th>Barn materials only?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>P: sloped board with two sizes of hole</td><td>188 of 200</td><td>2</td><td>4 min</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q: wire shaker tray</td><td>196 of 200</td><td>31</td><td>3 min</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>R: bought sizing machine</td><td>200 of 200</td><td>0</td><td>1 min</td><td>no</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design sorts most accurately? Is that enough to make it the best choice here? Explain.","Design Q is faster and more accurate than P. Name the criterion it fails and say why that matters more than speed here.","Which design best meets the criteria and fits inside the limits?"],key:["R sorts all 200 correctly, but it is not the best choice because it is bought rather than built from barn materials, which breaks one of the stated limits. (3-5-ETS1-2)","It bruises 31 apples out of 200. Bruised apples cannot be sold fresh, which is the whole point of sorting the big ones out, so speed does not make up for it. (3-5-ETS1-2)","P. It sorts 188 of 200, bruises only 2, beats hand sorting on time, and uses only what is in the barn. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The builders want to know whether a steeper board sorts better. They ran two boards side by side.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Board 1</th><th>Board 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>slope</td><td>gentle</td><td>steep</td></tr>
     <tr><td>hole sizes</td><td>60 mm and 75 mm</td><td>60 mm and 75 mm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>apples run through</td><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sorted correctly</td><td>81</td><td>94</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bruised</td><td>1</td><td>9</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>apples stalled on the board and had to be nudged</td><td>apples hit the end rail hard enough to bruise</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What did the builders hold the same, and what did they change? Why does that make the test fair?","If board 2 had also used bigger holes, could you still say the slope caused the better sorting? Explain.","Each board has a failure point in the last row. Name a change that would fix each one."],key:["They held the hole sizes and the number of apples the same and changed only the slope, so any difference in the results can be traced to the slope. (3-5-ETS1-3)","No. Two things would have changed, slope and hole size, so you could not tell which one caused the improvement. That is no longer a fair test. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Board 1 stalls, so raise the slope a little or smooth or wax the surface. Board 2 bruises at the rail, so pad the end rail, add a cloth catch, or shorten the drop. Any fix tied to the stated failure counts. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Nations Before 1492",short:"Before 1492",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing major pre-Columbian settlements by the materials, food, and shelter of each region",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four regions of North America before any European ship arrived, with what people there built with and ate.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Built with</th><th>Main foods</th></tr>
     <tr><td>desert Southwest</td><td>stone, adobe brick made of clay and straw</td><td>corn, beans, squash grown with careful irrigation</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Pacific Northwest coast</td><td>split cedar planks</td><td>salmon, shellfish, whale, berries</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Great Plains</td><td>poles and hides, taken down and moved</td><td>buffalo, wild plants</td></tr>
     <tr><td>eastern woodlands</td><td>bent saplings covered with bark</td><td>corn, beans, squash, deer, fish</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("cabin",70)}${F.icon("fish",70)}${F.icon("tent",70)}${F.icon("corn",70)}</div>`,qs:["Two regions on the chart grew the same three crops. Name the regions and the crops.","Only one region built homes meant to be taken apart and carried. Which one, and what about their food explains it?","Growing corn in the desert Southwest needed irrigation. What does that tell you about the people who did it?","Pick any two regions and name one thing that was the same and one thing that was different about how people lived."],key:["The desert Southwest and the eastern woodlands, both growing corn, beans, and squash. Partial: the two regions named with the crops missing, or the reverse, is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","The Great Plains. The buffalo herds they depended on moved across the grassland, so people followed the food and needed shelter they could carry. Partial: the region named with nothing said about the food is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","They had worked out how to move and store water across dry land, which takes planning, engineering, and organised work by many people together. Partial: saying they were clever with no account of planning or organised work is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Any supported pair. For example both the Southwest and the woodlands farmed corn, beans, and squash, but one built with stone and clay in a dry place and the other with saplings and bark in a forest. Partial: one of the same and the different given is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:33,title:"Why They Sailed",short:"Why Sail",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing early explorations of the Americas and the reasons behind each voyage",html:()=>`<p class="big">Explorers sailed for reasons their rulers cared about. Here are four voyages and what each one was after.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Explorer and year</th><th>For</th><th>Looking for</th><th>Where they went</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Juan Ponce de Leon, 1513</td><td>Spain</td><td>land and gold</td><td>the coast of Florida</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Ferdinand Magellan's expedition, 1519 to 1522</td><td>Spain</td><td>a western sea route to Asia</td><td>around the tip of South America and across the Pacific</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Hernando de Soto, 1539</td><td>Spain</td><td>gold</td><td>inland across the Southeast to the Mississippi River</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Henry Hudson, 1609</td><td>the Netherlands</td><td>a northern passage to Asia</td><td>up the river in what is now New York</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("compass",76)}${F.icon("anchor",76)}${F.icon("globe",76)}</div>`,qs:["Two of the four were hunting for a sea route to Asia. Which two, and which directions did they try?","Magellan's expedition was the first to sail all the way around the world. What did that prove about the shape and size of Earth?","De Soto's route crossed land, not open sea. What was he looking for, and did the chart say he found it?","Hudson sailed for the Netherlands, not for his own country, England. What does that tell you about how these voyages were paid for?"],key:["Magellan's expedition, which tried westward around South America, and Hudson, who tried a northern route. Partial: one of the two voyages named with its direction is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","That Earth is round and that the oceans connect, and that it is far larger than Europeans had thought, because the Pacific crossing took months longer than expected. Partial: naming the shape with nothing said about the size is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Gold. The chart says what he was looking for but not that he found it, and he did not. Partial: naming gold with no answer about whether he found it is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Rulers and merchant companies hired whoever had the skill, whatever country they came from. The money and the claim belonged to the country that paid. Partial: noting he sailed for another country with nothing said about who paid and who claimed is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the case. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing cooperation and conflict in the fur trade between American Indian nations and newcomers",html:()=>`<p class="big">The fur trade shows cooperation and conflict happening together.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What each side brought</th></tr>
     <tr><td>American Indian nations: knowledge of the land, trapping skill, canoe routes, prepared furs</td></tr>
     <tr><td>European traders: metal knives and pots, cloth, glass beads, guns</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Both sides wanted the trade. Over time, beaver grew scarce in the easiest places, and nations began hunting into land other nations used. European powers armed their trading partners, and old disagreements between nations grew sharper.</p>`,qs:["Name one thing each side gained from the fur trade.","This was cooperation. Explain how the same trade also led to conflict.","Why would arming a trading partner make conflict between nations more likely?","Who do you think had more power in this trade over the long run? Give a reason from the text."],key:["Nations gained metal tools, pots, cloth, and other goods. Traders gained furs they could sell in Europe for a great deal of money. Partial: one side's gain named is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","As beaver were trapped out near the posts, hunters had to go further, onto land other nations used, so competition over hunting ground grew. Partial: saying it caused fighting with no account of hunting ground running short is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","A disagreement that had been settled by talking or by small raids could now cause far greater harm, and a nation with guns had an advantage that pushed others to seek guns too. Partial: saying guns are dangerous with nothing said about what changed between nations is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Either answer, supported. The European traders controlled the supply of goods and decided who got guns, and the market was in Europe. Nations controlled the furs, the routes, and the skill, and could trade with a rival power instead. Partial: a side chosen with no reason from the text is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:34,title:"Colonial Work and Growing Anger",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:175,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing the economic and social institutions of the three colonial regions",html:()=>`<p class="big">The thirteen colonies are often grouped into three regions because the work and the way of life differed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Land and climate</th><th>Main work</th><th>Who did the work</th></tr>
     <tr><td>New England</td><td>rocky soil, short summers, good harbours</td><td>fishing, shipbuilding, timber, small family farms</td><td>families, apprentices, some enslaved people</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Middle colonies</td><td>good soil, long rivers</td><td>wheat and other grain, milling, trade</td><td>families, indentured servants, enslaved people</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Southern colonies</td><td>warm, long growing season</td><td>tobacco, rice, indigo on large plantations</td><td>mostly enslaved Africans</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">An indentured servant agreed to work without pay for a set number of years, usually in exchange for the cost of the voyage, and was free afterward. An enslaved person was held as property for life, with no agreement and no end date, and their children were held too.</p>
     <p class="big">The religious institutions of a region differed as much as the work did.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Main religious institutions</th></tr>
     <tr><td>New England</td><td>Puritan congregations, with a meetinghouse at the centre of each town and the minister paid by a town tax</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Middle colonies</td><td>Quakers in Pennsylvania, German Lutheran and Reformed congregations, Presbyterians, Catholics and others, side by side with no single group in charge</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Southern colonies</td><td>the Anglican Church of England, established by law in Virginia and several others, with parish taxes collected from everyone</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why did New England build ships and fish rather than run large plantations?","Name the difference between an indentured servant and an enslaved person.","The Middle colonies were called the breadbasket. Which column of the first chart explains that name?","Using the second chart, name the main religious institution of New England. Name the main one of the southern colonies. Then say what was different about the Middle colonies."],key:["Rocky soil and short summers made large crops impossible, while good harbours and timber nearby made fishing and shipbuilding pay. Partial: naming the poor soil with nothing said about harbours or timber is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","An indentured servant had an agreement, worked a set number of years, and then was free. An enslaved person was held as property for life with no agreement and no end, and their children were held as property too. Partial: describing one of the two with nothing said about the other is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","The land and climate column: good soil and long rivers meant large grain harvests that could be milled and shipped. Partial: naming the column with no account of what it says is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","New England: Puritan congregations, with a meetinghouse at the centre of the town and the minister paid by a town tax. Southern colonies: the Anglican Church of England, established by law and supported by parish taxes collected from everyone. Middle colonies: many groups side by side, Quakers, Lutheran and Reformed congregations, Presbyterians and Catholics, with no single group established in charge. Full credit needs all three. Partial: two of the three regions described is approaching. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining causes of the American Revolution beyond taxes",html:()=>`<p class="big">Not every cause was a tax. Four more decisions from London that angered colonists.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Decision</th><th>Why colonists objected</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1763</td><td>the Proclamation forbade settlement west of the Appalachian Mountains</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1765</td><td>the Quartering Act required colonists to house and supply British soldiers</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1767</td><td>customs officers were given broad warrants to search warehouses and homes</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1774</td><td>some trials were moved out of the colony where the event happened</td><td>?</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why would colonists object to being told not to settle west of the mountains?","Why would housing soldiers in your own home feel different from paying a tax?","Colonists called the broad search warrants a violation of their rights as Englishmen. What right did they think was being broken?","Moving a trial out of the colony where it happened troubled colonists deeply. Why?"],key:["Many had already claimed or bought land out there, or hoped to, and the line cut off land they counted on. It was also a decision about their future made by a government they had not elected. Partial: naming the lost land with nothing said about who made the decision is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","A tax takes money, but quartering puts a stranger inside your house and takes your food and your privacy with no say and no end date. Partial: saying it is worse with no account of what quartering actually took is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","The right not to have your home or property searched without a specific reason and a specific warrant. Partial: naming privacy with nothing said about needing a specific reason and warrant is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","A jury from your own colony, made of neighbours who knew the situation, was replaced by strangers far away, so colonists believed a fair trial was no longer possible. Partial: saying it was unfair with nothing said about the jury is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the notes. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the course of the war through its hardest winter and the terms that ended it",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Continental Army was short of almost everything for most of the war.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Winter 1777 to 1778, Valley Forge</th></tr>
     <tr><td>about 11,000 soldiers camped in log huts for six months</td></tr>
     <tr><td>shortages of shoes, blankets, and food; roughly 2,000 died of illness and cold</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Congress could ask the states for money but could not make them pay</td></tr>
     <tr><td>in the spring the army drilled under a new training plan and marched out better organised</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two reasons the army was short of supplies.","Congress could ask the states for money but could not make them pay. Why would that make supplying an army so hard?","The army left Valley Forge in better shape than it arrived. What happened during the winter to cause that?","Why would a long war favour the American side even though Britain had the stronger army?"],key:["Any two: Congress had little money and could not tax, roads and wagons were poor, British forces held major ports, and farmers were often paid in money that lost its value. Partial: one reason named is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","If several states sent little or nothing, there was no way to make up the gap, so food and clothing simply did not arrive no matter what Congress ordered. Partial: saying Congress was weak with no account of what happened when states paid nothing is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","The soldiers were trained and drilled through the winter under a new plan, so they could move and fight as organised units rather than as separate groups. Partial: saying they got through the winter with nothing said about the training is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Britain had to ship everything across an ocean and hold ground far from home, which cost enormous sums, while the Americans only had to avoid losing badly enough to quit. Time and distance worked against Britain. Partial: naming the distance with nothing said about what the Americans needed only to do is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:35,title:"A New Government, A Growing Country",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the compromises at the Constitutional Convention and why they were needed",html:()=>`<p class="big">At the 1787 convention the states disagreed about how many votes each should get in the new Congress.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it proposed</th><th>Who liked it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Virginia Plan</td><td>votes in Congress based on a state's population</td><td>large states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>New Jersey Plan</td><td>every state gets the same number of votes</td><td>small states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the Great Compromise</td><td>two houses: the House of Representatives by population, the Senate with two from every state</td><td>enough of both to pass</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The delegates also disagreed about how enslaved people would be counted when working out a state's population. They settled on counting three out of every five, which gave slaveholding states more votes in Congress without giving enslaved people any rights at all.</p>`,qs:["Why did large states and small states want different plans?","Explain how the Great Compromise gave each side part of what it wanted.","What does compromise mean, and why was one needed at all here?","The counting of enslaved people was a compromise too. Who gained from it, and who gained nothing?"],key:["Large states thought votes should match how many people a state had. Small states feared being outvoted on everything and wanted equal votes. Partial: one side's position given is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","The House is by population, which the large states wanted, and the Senate gives every state two seats, which the small states wanted. A law has to pass both. Partial: one of the two houses explained is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","A compromise is an agreement where each side gives up part of what it wanted. Without one, neither group would have signed and there would have been no Constitution. Partial: defining compromise with no account of why one was needed is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Slaveholding states gained extra seats in the House and extra weight in presidential elections. Enslaved people gained nothing: they were counted for the state's power but had no vote, no freedom, and no rights under the document. Partial: naming who gained with nothing said about who gained nothing is approaching. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how a western territory became a state and what drew settlers to it",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Northwest Ordinance of 1787 set the steps for turning western land into new states.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>land was surveyed and divided into square townships for sale</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>Congress appointed a governor while few settlers lived there</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>at 5,000 free adult men, settlers could elect their own assembly</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>at 60,000 people, the territory could apply to join as a state, equal to the others</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The ordinance also banned slavery in the Northwest Territory and promised that American Indian land would not be taken without consent, a promise that was broken again and again.</p>`,qs:["Name the two population numbers in the steps and say what each one unlocked.","Why would a surveyed grid of square townships make land easier to sell to settlers far away?","What was the economic reason a family would move into a new territory?","The ordinance promised American Indian land would not be taken without consent. What actually happened?"],key:["5,000 free adult men allowed settlers to elect their own assembly, and 60,000 people allowed the territory to apply for statehood. Partial: one of the two numbers matched to what it unlocked is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","A buyer could be told exactly which numbered square they were getting without travelling there first, and the boundaries were already clear, so land could be sold by mail and disputes were fewer. Partial: saying it was tidier with nothing said about buying without travelling there is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Land was cheap or free compared with the East, the soil was good, and a family could own a farm they could never have afforded back home. Partial: saying they wanted land with no comparison to the East is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","The promise was broken. Settlers moved onto land anyway, treaties were signed under pressure and then ignored, and nations were pushed further west over the following decades. Partial: saying the promise was broken with no account of how is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating states in the Northeast and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("map",96)}${F.icon("flag",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">Questions about the Northeast and about capitals in general.</p>`,qs:["Name the six New England states.","What is the capital of Massachusetts, and what is the capital of Pennsylvania?","Which is the smallest state by area, and which Northeast state borders Canada along its whole northern edge?","New York City is not the capital of New York. What is?","How many states are there in all, and how many of them are in New England?"],key:["Maine, New Hampshire, Vermont, Massachusetts, Rhode Island, Connecticut. Partial: four or five of the six named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Boston, Massachusetts and Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. Note if the student names Philadelphia, which is the usual slip. Partial: one of the two capitals right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Rhode Island is the smallest by area. Maine borders Canada along its northern edge. Accept Vermont or New Hampshire if the student explains they also touch Canada. Partial: one of the two answered is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Albany (HSS-5.9)","50 states in all, 6 of them in New England. Partial: one of the two numbers right is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=STORY.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[0].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level story read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Prove It Twice",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use evidence from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>What kind of worker is Priya? Say your idea in one sentence.</li><li>Give two pieces of evidence from two different parts of the story that support your idea. Tell how each one supports it.</li></ol>${F.passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Makes a claim about Priya that fits the story, such as: she solves problems on her own, she stays calm under pressure, or she is responsible. (RL.6.1, analysis)","Cites two pieces of evidence from different parts and explains each, such as: she knelt down and looked under the tub when she wanted to run for help, she scraped the skins loose with a spoon, she said, “So I fixed it,” and Mrs. Okafor put her at the top of next Saturday’s list. (RL.6.1, cites textual evidence)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Build the Case",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use evidence from the text.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Give one reason grafting matters to apple growers. Back it up with evidence from the text.</li><li>Give a second, different reason from another paragraph. Back it up with evidence too.</li></ol>${F.passage(GRAFT,GRAFT_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Reason 1 with evidence, such as: it is the only way to get the same kind of apple, because a seed will not grow the same tree and the fruit is usually small and sour (paragraph 1), or the top is a copy of the tree the twig came from (paragraph 3). (RI.6.1)","Reason 2 from a different paragraph with evidence, such as: growers can choose how tall the trees get, and short trees are easier to pick and more fit in a row (paragraph 3). The student ties each piece of evidence to the reason. (RI.6.1)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Fraction Crates",std:["6.G.A.2"],needs:["5.MD.C.5"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper. Then tell your teacher your answers.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>An apple crate is 2 1/2 feet long, 2 feet wide, and 1 1/2 feet tall. What is its volume?</li><li>A small cube has edges that are 1/3 foot long. How many of these small cubes fill a box that is 1 foot long, 1 foot wide, and 1 foot tall?</li><li>What is the volume of one of the small cubes in number 2?</li></ol>`,key:["7 1/2 cubic feet, from 2 1/2 × 2 × 1 1/2 (5 × 1 1/2). (6.G.A.2)","27 small cubes: 3 fit along each edge, and 3 × 3 × 3 = 27. (6.G.A.2)","1/27 cubic foot, from 1/3 × 1/3 × 1/3. (6.G.A.2)"]},15:{id:"L15",title:"Level Up: Pronoun Patrol",std:["L.6.1"],needs:["L.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the right way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix it: “Me and Priya carried the crates.”</li><li>Fix it: “The owner thanked Theo and I.”</li><li>This sentence is not clear: “When Priya handed Nora the basket, she dropped it.” Who dropped the basket? Say the sentence so that a listener can tell.</li></ol>`,key:["Priya and I carried the crates. (L.6.1, subject pronoun)","The owner thanked Theo and me. (L.6.1, object pronoun)","We cannot tell who she is. A clear sentence names the person, such as: Priya dropped the basket when she handed it to Nora. Or: Nora dropped the basket when Priya handed it to her. (L.6.1, fixing a vague pronoun)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","W.5.8","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function pvChart(){const head=["Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones",".","Tenths","Hundredths","Thousandths"],vals=["1,000","100","10","1",".","0.1","0.01","0.001"],fr=["","","","",".","1/10","1/100","1/1000"];const cell=(c,i,tag2,extra)=>{const dec=i>=4;const st=`padding:${tag2==="th"?"8px 4px":"10px 4px"};text-align:center;${i===4?"width:20px;font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:700;":""}${dec&&i>4?"background:var(--sea-soft);":""}${extra||""}`;return`<${tag2} style="${st}">${c}</${tag2}>`};const row=(arr,tag2,extra)=>`<tr>${arr.map((c,i)=>cell(c,i,tag2,extra)).join("")}</tr>`;return`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="width:100%;font-size:.95rem">${row(head,"th","font-size:.74rem;white-space:nowrap;padding-left:1px;padding-right:1px;")}${row(vals,"td","font-weight:700;")}${row(fr,"td","font-size:.85rem;")}${row(["","","","",".","","",""],"td","height:54px;font-size:1.8rem;font-weight:700;")}</table></div><p class="small">The bottom row is for digits. Each place to the left is worth 10 times as much. Each place to the right is worth 1/10 as much. The last place, <b>thousandths</b>, is the third one after the point.</p>`}function strips(){const dens=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8],W=600,h=38;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+8} ${dens.length*(h+6)+4}" width="100%" style="max-width:640px" role="img" aria-label="Fraction strips for halves, thirds, fourths, fifths, sixths, and eighths">`;dens.forEach((d,r)=>{for(let k=0;k<d;k++){const x=4+k*W/d,y=2+r*(h+6);s+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${W/d}" height="${h}" fill="${r%2?"var(--sea-soft)":"var(--surface)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x+W/d/2}" y="${y+h/2}" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="17" fill="var(--ink)">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</text>`}});return s+"</svg>"}const frames=list=>`<ul class="starters">${list.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function sentHtml(st){const has=c=>st&&st.std&&st.std.includes(c);if(has("W.5.3"))return frames(["It all started when ____.","First, ____.","Just then, ____.","“____,” I said.","In the end, ____."]);if(has("W.5.2"))return frames(["Apples are ____.","One interesting fact is ____.","Another fact is ____.","For example, ____.","Now you know ____."]);if(has("SL.5.4"))return frames(["I think ____.","My first reason is ____. For example, ____.","Another reason is ____.","That is why ____."]);if(has("RI.5.9"))return frames(["Text A says ____.","Text ____ adds that ____.","Putting these together, ____."]);return frames(["The text says, “____.”","I know ____ because the text says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."])}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.NF.B.4","5.OA.A.1","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const row=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===row&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[row,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart to thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.1","5.NBT.A.3"],html:pvChart},strips:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.4"],html:strips},formula:{name:"Volume formula card",std:["5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>V = length × width × height</li><li>V = B × h (B is the area of the base)</li><li>Two boxes joined together: find each volume, then add.</li><li>Volume is measured in cubic units.</li></ul>`},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3","SL.5.4","RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RI.5.9"],html:sentHtml},figs:{name:"Simile and metaphor card",std:["RL.5.4"],html:()=>`<ul class="starters"><li>Simile: compares two things with like or as. “Her smile was like sunshine.”</li><li>Metaphor: says one thing is another thing. “The classroom was a zoo.”</li><li>Ask: What two things are compared? How are they alike?</li></ul>`}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["5.NF.B.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products and quotients. Rate on whether the student sets up the problem and explains it."},{t:"pv",std:["5.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The place value chart states the 10 times and 1/10 rule, which is what 5.NBT.A.1 checks. Count this stop as supported, not independent."},{t:"pv",std:["5.NBT.A.3"],msg:"The place value chart names each decimal place, so count reading and writing the decimals as supported."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.B.3"],msg:"With fraction strips the student can see the size of each share. Rate on whether the student can name the share as a division problem and a fraction."},{t:"strips",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.4"],msg:"Fraction strips show a common unit without the student having to find one. Note whether the student chose the common denominator or read it off the strips."},{t:"formula",std:["5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The formula card gives the volume formulas and the add-the-parts rule. Count those answers as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.1","W.5.2","W.5.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking or time words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.4"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the order of the talk and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RI.5.9"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote or to name the text. Rate on the accuracy of the quote and the facts."},{t:"figs",std:["RL.5.4"],msg:"The card tells what a simile and a metaphor are, so count the simile or metaphor question as supported."},{t:"passage",std:["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","RI.5.9","RL.6.1","RI.6.1"],msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"If the sources were read to the student, the notes still count for W.5.8, but write down that the sources were read aloud."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
